                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "41934397",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198030669842",
                            "num_reviews": 30,
                            "playtime_forever": 151,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 151,
                            "last_played": 1525864171
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I really wanted to like this game, but unfortunately it just isn't good.\n\nEach case is just a collection of up to 4 suspects and up to 4 witnesses. You can talk to all of them (which gives you a line of written dialogue) and you can investigate the witnesses (which gives you a clue or nothing). The problem is that everything takes time, especially investigating. Since you get no useful information on the suspects, you have nothing to base your decisions on. Basically all you can do is memorize which investigations are useful and do those on your second or third playthrough. You're not a sleuth, you're playing Memory. The pictures of Old Hollywood actors for the people are fitting, but since they are assigned randomly each time you start a case, you cannot use them to remember who is who. You better write down the names.\n\nAnother problem is that some cases can only be solved by asking one of your sources of inside information. However, those will stop helping you unless you deliberately drop certain cases. So you can choose to lose a case now or lose some cases later. Great fun! I guess the developers were going for moral decisions like in \"Papers, Please!\", but in that game you just had to look the other way for a minute of your day, not deliberately screw up a case that took you a week to solve.\n\n\nEdit - my answers to the developer's response:\n\n> But the faces are assigned randomly IN PURPOSE so that you CANNOT remember who is who.\n\nThe problem is not that the faces are randomly assigned, but that there is no way to determine, which investigation could be useful. More randomization compounds this problem.\n\n> I wasn’t going for moral decisions but for strategic ones.\n\nYes, you can decide which people to please - for the current case. But annoying your internal sources will make later cases impossible to solve. That is the problem.",
                        "timestamp_created": 1525865069,
                        "timestamp_updated": 1528351258,
                        "voted_up": false,
                        "votes_up": 24,
                        "votes_funny": 3,
                        "weighted_vote_score": 0.7437747120857239,
                        "comment_count": 0,
                        "steam_purchase": true,
                        "received_for_free": false,
                        "written_during_early_access": false,
                        "developer_response": "Hi\nSorry you didn’t like the game. \n\nI just wanted to mention that you seem to have missed the point of the game in some areas. \n\nFor example you say: “The pictures of Old Hollywood actors for the people are fitting, but since they are assigned randomly each time you start a case you cannot use them to remember who is who. You better write down the names.”\nBut the faces are assigned randomly IN PURPOSE so that you CANNOT remember who is who. If I ever thought that you would write down the names to remember who is who I would also assign the names randomly.\n\nThen you say:  ”…some cases can only be solved by asking one of your sources of inside information. However, those will stop helping you unless you deliberately drop certain cases. So you can choose to lose a case now or lose some cases later. Great fun! I guess the developers were going for moral decisions like in \"Papers, Please!”\n I wasn’t going for moral decisions but for strategic ones. If you can afford to lose a case (like you have a good record and the Captain is happy with your job) or you just don’t want to destroy your stats, or you can drop a case and spend time with the family so the wife is happy again etc.",
                        "timestamp_dev_responded": 1528324701,
                        "primarily_steam_deck": false,
                        "refunded": false
                    }
